= Fuling (Poria, 茯苓)

_Scope: this entry treats the dried sclerotium of *Poria cocos* (Schw.) F.A. Wolf — in current mycology *Wolfiporia cocos* (F.A. Wolf) Ryvarden & Gilb., family Polyporaceae — the Chinese drug *茯苓 (fúlíng)*, together with its five classical part-derived drug-forms (茯苓皮, 白茯苓, 赤茯苓, 茯神, 茯神木), which Chinese pharmacy treats as distinct drugs rather than grades. Status: deeply classical in Chinese medicine — *Shennong Bencao Jing* superior grade (上品), and by formula-frequency one of the two or three most-used drugs in the entire pharmacopoeia. Not classical in Ayurveda, Greco-Roman medicine, or Tibb: an East Asian pine-forest fungus that never entered those corpora, so those sections are reasoned extension, flagged. Two disambiguations: (1) the English trade name "China root" historically denotes *Smilax china*, a different drug of 16th-c. European pharmacy, and should not be used for poria; (2) the identical fungus occurs in North America as "tuckahoe" / "Indian bread," an independent subsistence-food use, not a transmitted tradition._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / mycological:* _Poria cocos_ (Schw.) F.A. Wolf, 1915 — the pharmacognostic binomial, retained in the _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ and in nearly all materia medica literature; current mycological placement is *_Wolfiporia cocos_* (F.A. Wolf) Ryvarden & Gilb., 1984 (the name _W. extensa_ also appears), family Polyporaceae, the bracket-fungus family. *The organism and the drug:* a wood-decay fungus that colonizes the roots and stumps of pines — in China principally _Pinus massoniana_ and _P. tabuliformis_ — and forms a large subterranean *sclerotium*, a hardened mass of mycelium and stored polysaccharide (the fungal analogue of a storage tuber) that can range from fist-sized to, exceptionally, tens of kilograms. *The drug is this sclerotium*: not a fruiting body, not a root, not a plant part. Harvest is from July to September; the sclerotium is sweated, dried, and divided into the part-forms below.#footnote[Taxonomy: Ryvarden & Gilbertson's 1984 transfer to _Wolfiporia_; _Poria cocos_ persists as the pharmacognostic name in the _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ and in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. Host-tree association, sclerotium biology, and harvest/processing: standard mycological and Chinese pharmacognosy references.]

*Chinese:* *茯苓 (fúlíng)* — attested from the *神農本草經 (Shennong Bencao Jing)*, Han period, in the *上品 (superior grade)*, the longevity-and-constitution class. *Etymology, and a well-documented classical folk account:* the name appears in early sources also as *伏靈 (fúlíng, "crouching numen" / "hidden spirit")* and *伏苓*, and the character 茯 is 艸 ("plant") over 伏 ("to crouch, lie hidden, submit") — naming the fungus's concealed subterranean growth. The classical lore, discussed at length by *Li Shizhen* in the *本草綱目 (Bencao Gangmu,* 1578), holds that the sclerotium is the *congealed vital essence of the pine* sinking underground over long years — the pine's _shén_ or _jīng_ gathered and hardened beneath it — and classical herb-finding lore (recorded in the *史記 (Shiji)* "龜策列傳" chapter and repeated in later materia medica) paired *伏靈* below with *菟絲 (Cuscuta,* dodder) visible above as the surface sign of a sclerotium beneath. *Flagged as folk and quasi-mythological etymology, not mycology:* the modern account is a saprophytic/parasitic basidiomycete decaying pine root-wood, with no relation to congealed resin or pine spirit. The folk account is nonetheless load-bearing for the tradition, since it underwrites the drug's conceptual link to the pine's own longevity symbolism — and the *茯神* form's spirit-calming reservation (below) follows directly from it.#footnote[茯苓 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (上品). The 伏靈/伏苓 orthographies, the pine-essence account, and the 菟絲-above/伏靈-below herb-finding lore: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, 茯苓 entry, which collates earlier authorities including Tao Hongjing; the _Shiji_ 龜策列傳 reference is cited in that tradition and should be verified against a specific _Shiji_ edition. Flagged explicitly as classical folk etymology, not modern mycological fact.]

*The classical drug-forms — a five-way part-split, and one of this book's clearest saṃskāra systems.* Chinese pharmacy divides the single sclerotium into drugs with distinct properties and indications, on the principle that a part's *position* in the organism parallels its clinical target in the body (the same reasoning that gives 桑葉/桑枝/桑白皮 their separate scopes):
- *茯苓个 (fúlíng gè)* — the entire undivided sclerotium, as harvested.
- *白茯苓 / 茯苓塊 (bái fúlíng / fúlíng kuài)* — the *white inner flesh*, cut into blocks or slices; the standard drug, strongest on *健脾* (Spleen-strengthening) and fluid-stabilizing.
- *赤茯苓 (chì fúlíng)* — the *pinkish-brown layer* beneath the rind; read as *more draining and less tonifying*, preferred for damp-heat and for 利水 where no Spleen support is wanted.
- *茯苓皮 (fúlíng pí)* — the *outer rind*, peeled off and used separately for *superficial/skin edema* (皮水, "skin-water") — the part-position principle at its most explicit: the skin of the drug treats the water in the skin.
- *茯神 (fúshén, "poria-spirit")* — the portion that has *grown around and enclosed a pine root*, with the root core embedded; reserved for *寧心安神* (calming the Heart, settling the spirit) — insomnia, palpitations, anxiety. The reservation follows the folk etymology above: the part holding the pine's own core is the part that settles the _shén_.
- *茯神木 (fúshén mù)* — the *embedded pine-root core itself*, extracted from 茯神 and used as a minor drug for Liver-wind and limb spasm.
- *朱茯神 / 朱砂拌茯神* — historically, 茯神 *dusted with cinnabar (朱砂, mercuric sulfide)* to intensify the spirit-calming action. *This form is now to be avoided:* cinnabar is a mercury compound, and the mercury-containing processed forms of Chinese pharmacy are restricted in modern practice and prohibited in many jurisdictions. Recorded for textual completeness, flagged as *not to be used*.#footnote[Drug-form distinctions: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ monographs for 茯苓, 茯苓皮, and 茯神 as separately standardized drugs; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The part-position/clinical-target reasoning is standard Bencao pharmacology. The cinnabar-dusted 朱茯神 is classical but is a mercury preparation: modern regulatory restriction and the toxicological case against cinnabar-containing preparations are well established — flagged as not for use, not merely cautioned.]

*The paired-drug contrast — 猪苓 (zhūlíng):* classical pharmacy pairs fúlíng with *猪苓 (zhūlíng,* _Polyporus umbellatus_), another sclerotium-forming polypore. The contrast is textually fixed and clinically load-bearing: *zhūlíng drains water more strongly but has no tonifying action*, while *fúlíng drains more gently and simultaneously supports the Spleen*. The two appear together in 五苓散 and 豬苓湯, and the distinction is the standard teaching example of "draining with support" versus "draining alone."

*English:* *Poria* (the usual English-language TCM name); *fuling* (pinyin, increasingly standard); *"hoelen"* (an older pharmacy name, from the Japanese reading). *Two naming cautions:* (1) *"China root"* is best avoided — in 16th–18th c. European pharmacy _radix chinae_ / "China root" denotes *_Smilax china_*, a sarsaparilla-class drug imported for syphilis and gout, an entirely different substance; (2) *"tuckahoe"* and *"Indian bread"* name the same fungal species in North America but a different, unrelated use (below).

*American convergence — tuckahoe / Indian bread:* _Wolfiporia cocos_ occurs naturally in the southeastern and mid-Atlantic United States, where subterranean sclerotia were dug and eaten as a starchy famine-and-subsistence food by some Native American peoples and by early settlers. *The name is a secondary transfer:* Algonquian _tuckahoe_ properly denotes the arum root _Peltandra virginica_, extended by settlers to any underground "Indian bread," including this fungus. *This is independent convergent ethnomycology, not shared tradition* — the use was alimentary, with none of the elaborated part-splitting, channel-attribution, or formula pharmacology of the Chinese drug, and no evidence of transmission in either direction.#footnote[American _Wolfiporia cocos_ / tuckahoe subsistence use and the _Peltandra virginica_ name-transfer: 19th–20th c. American folk-botanical and mycological literature, and standard North American ethnobotanical compilations. Flagged as independent convergence; "China root" (_Smilax china_) as a distinct European drug: Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_.]

*Japanese:* *茯苓 (ブクリョウ, bukuryō)* — standard in Kampo, where fúlíng-containing formulas (_Hochuekkito_, _Rikkunshito_, _Goreisan_, _Keishibukuryogan_) are among the most prescribed. *Korean:* *복령 (bongnyeong)*. *Vietnamese:* *bạch phục linh*.

*Premium origins:* *雲苓 (Yúnlíng)* — Yunnan material, the traditional premium grade; modern production is dominated by Hubei, Anhui, Guangxi, Fujian, and Yunnan, largely from *inoculated pine stumps/logs* rather than wild harvest, which has largely ceased to supply the market.

*Sanskrit:* *no classical name* — see the Ayurveda section. *Arabic / Persian:* *no classical name* — see the Tibb section. Neither tradition received this fungus; modern Arabic and Hindi herbal commerce, where it appears at all, uses transliterations of the Chinese or English names.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

茯苓 is *deeply classical and unusually ubiquitous*: a _Shennong Bencao Jing_ 上品 drug present in a very large share of classical formulas across categories — qi-tonifying, damp-draining, phlegm-transforming, spirit-calming — precisely because it is gentle enough to combine with nearly anything. Its classical reputation is *平和 (pínghé,* harmonious, even-tempered).

*Property (性 xìng):* Neutral (平).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘), *Bland (淡)*. *The bland flavour is the pharmacologically operative one and deserves note:* 淡 is one of the two flavours (with 澀, astringent) added to the canonical five, and the classical principle *淡味滲泄 ("the bland flavour percolates and discharges")* holds that blandness itself drains dampness through urination. Bland is thus not an absence of flavour but a positive pharmacological category — *and it has no counterpart among the six Ayurvedic rasas*, a genuine structural divergence between the two systems, taken up in the Ayurveda section below.

*Channels (歸經 guījīng):* Heart (心), Spleen (脾), Kidney (腎); Lung (肺) in some sources. The Heart entry is carried principally by the *茯神* form.

*Actions and indications:*
- *利水滲濕 (lì shuǐ shèn shī)* — promotes urination and leaches out dampness: edema, scanty difficult urination, abdominal fullness from fluid accumulation, *痰飲* (phlegm-fluid) patterns. The canonical formula is *五苓散 (Wǔ Líng Sǎn,* Five-Ingredient Powder with Poria) from Zhang Zhongjing's *傷寒論 (Shāng Hán Lùn,* ~200 CE) — with 豬苓, 澤瀉, 白朮, 桂枝 — for water accumulation with exterior signs and thirst with inability to hold fluids. *豬苓湯* and *真武湯* (with aconite and ginger, for yang-vacuity water flooding) extend the register to hotter and colder patterns respectively.
- *健脾 (jiàn pí)* — strengthens the Spleen: the function that distinguishes fúlíng from a pure diuretic (cf. 猪苓 above). The archetype is *四君子湯 (Sì Jūnzǐ Tāng,* Four Gentlemen Decoction) — 人參, 白朮, 茯苓, 炙甘草 — from the Song *太平惠民和劑局方* (1108), the foundational Spleen-qi tonic and the structural parent of an entire family: *六君子湯* (adding 陳皮, 半夏), *異功散*, *參苓白朮散* (adding 山藥, 蓮子, 薏苡仁 — see `coix.typ`), *補中益氣湯* in part.
- *化痰 / 滲濕化痰* — transforms phlegm by draining its source: fúlíng is the standard companion to 半夏 and 陳皮 in *二陳湯 (Èr Chén Tāng)*, the base phlegm-damp formula, on the classical principle that phlegm arises from undrained damp and that treating the Spleen treats the source (*治痰先治脾*). *苓桂朮甘湯* treats phlegm-fluid in the chest with dizziness and palpitations.
- *寧心安神 (níng xīn ān shén)* — calms the Heart, settles the spirit: insomnia, palpitations, anxiety, especially of *Heart-Spleen dual vacuity* type. Principally the *茯神* form. The canonical formula is *歸脾湯 (Guī Pí Tāng,* Restore the Spleen Decoction; Yan Yonghe, *濟生方*, 13th c.), combining Spleen-qi tonics with Heart-blood nourishers and spirit-settlers (龍眼肉, 酸棗仁, 遠志); *酸棗仁湯* from the *金匱要略* pairs fúlíng with sour jujube seed for vexed insomnia.
- *The "tonify-and-drain" architecture (補中有瀉).* Fúlíng is one of the *三瀉* (three drainers: 茯苓, 澤瀉, 丹皮) that balance the *三補* (three tonics: 熟地, 山茱萸, 山藥) in *六味地黃丸 (Liù Wèi Dì Huáng Wán)* — Qian Yi, *小兒藥證直訣*, 1119 (see `rehmannia.typ`). This paired structure, in which drainers are built into a tonifying formula so that supplementation does not become stagnation, is among the signature architectural principles of classical formula design, and fúlíng is its most-used instrument.

*Cautions:* fúlíng is among the mildest drugs in the pharmacy and carries no classical 十八反/十九畏 incompatibility. The real caution is *logical rather than toxicological*: a draining drug used where there is nothing to drain — frank *yin or fluid vacuity without damp accumulation*, or copious clear urination — can over time deplete fluids further; dose and duration, not the drug, are the issue. Classical sources also caution against its use where urination is already excessive or where there is *氣虛下陷* (sinking qi) with prolapse, the descending/draining tendency being unhelpful there.#footnote[茯苓 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (上品); _Shāng Hán Lùn_ (Zhang Zhongjing, ~200 CE) for 五苓散, 真武湯, 苓桂朮甘湯; _Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_ for 酸棗仁湯; _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (1108) for 四君子湯 and 二陳湯's lineage; Yan Yonghe, _濟生方_ (13th c.) for 歸脾湯; Qian Yi, _小兒藥證直訣_ (1119) for 六味地黃丸 and the 三補三瀉 structure. The 淡味滲泄 principle and 治痰先治脾 maxim are standard Bencao-and-formula pharmacology. Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Poria cocos _is an East Asian pine-forest fungus with no entry in Caraka, Suśruta, or the nighaṇṭus, and no Sanskrit name; fungi in general are sparsely and mostly unfavourably treated in the saṃhitā dietetics (_chatraka_/_kavaka_ mushrooms being classed as heavy and largely unsuitable), so there is not even a congenial classical category to receive it. The reading below applies dravyaguna principles by extension, in the register of the other reasoned-only East-Asian entries (`green-tea.typ`, `coix.typ`), and one structural divergence is flagged rather than papered over: the drug's operative Chinese flavour, 淡 (bland), has *no counterpart among the six rasas*._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet), *faint to the point of near-tastelessness*. The Chinese 淡 (bland) — a positive pharmacological category under the principle _淡味滲泄_, "the bland percolates and discharges" — maps onto *no* Ayurvedic rasa; the nearest dravyaguna devices are *अव्यक्त रस (avyakta rasa,* indistinct taste) and *अल्प रस (alpa rasa,* scant taste), both of which describe the absence of a flavour rather than attributing a drainage action to it. *This is a real structural asymmetry between the two pharmacologies*, not a translation difficulty, and it is worth stating plainly: the Chinese system has a flavour-category for "drains by being bland" and the Ayurvedic system does not.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry — mildly), Mṛdu (मृदु, soft), Sūkṣma (सूक्ष्म, subtle — the capacity to reach and open the _srotas_).

*Reasoned vīrya:* *Anuṣṇāśīta (अनुष्णाशीत, "neither heating nor cooling")* — the dravyaguna category for temperate potency, and the exact rendering of the Chinese 平 (neutral). The assignment is unusually well-founded for a reasoned reading, since both systems have an explicit neutral category and the drug sits in it in its home tradition.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Prabhāva:* *None classically* — no classical entry exists. If one were singled out, the candidate is the drug's defining paradox: *it drains without depleting*, combining _mūtrala_ (diuretic) action with _balya_ (strengthening) support in a single substance — the property that makes it the instrument of the Chinese *補中有瀉* architecture and that distinguishes it from 猪苓, the pure drainer. A substance that reduces excess while supporting the tissue it acts on is not predictable from light-dry-bland-neutral parameters, which is precisely the prabhāva criterion. Flagged as *interpretive*, not classical.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Kapha-śāmaka (the governing effect — _kleda_/damp-reducing, laghu-rūkṣa); Pitta-neutral; Vāta-neutral at ordinary dose but *Vāta-vardhaka in excess or in dry constitutions* (any sustained _mūtrala_ action depletes _rasa-dhātu_ and aggravates Vāta). The near-neutrality on two doṣas is the dravyaguna statement of why the drug combines universally — it is as close to *tridoṣa-sāmya* (doṣa-indifferent) as a draining drug can be.

*Reasoned karma:*
- _Mūtrala_ — diuretic (利水).
- _Śothahara_ — reduces edema and swelling (the 消腫 register).
- _Kleda-śoṣaṇa / kapha-hara_ — dries excess tissue-moisture and mucus (滲濕化痰).
- _Balya, agni-vardhaka (mild)_ — strengthening and digestion-supporting without heating (健脾).
- _Nidrā-janana, manaḥ-praśamana_ — sleep-promoting and mind-settling (寧心安神 — the _fúshén_ register).
- _Hṛdya (mild)_ — cardiotonic, by way of fluid-burden reduction and the Heart-channel entry rather than as a direct cardiac action.
- _Srotośodhana_ — channel-clearing; the dravyaguna rendering of the drug's role as an adjuvant that keeps tonifying formulas from stagnating.#footnote[Reasoned placement renders the classical Chinese 性味歸經 and indication-set onto dravyaguna axes; it is *not* a classical Ayurvedic entry — flagged. The _anuṣṇāśīta_ (neutral-vīrya) and _avyakta-rasa_ devices are genuine dravyaguna categories (P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_), used here as the closest available renderings. The saṃhitā treatment of mushrooms (_chatraka_/_kavaka_) as heavy and generally unsuitable food is in the Caraka and Suśruta dietetic sections — noted to show that no congenial classical category exists for a medicinal fungus. _Śothahara_ is added to the glossary with this entry.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha for P. cocos — extensions of conditional rules plus modern pharmacological cautions; tags: S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk/regional, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(extended) Conditional:* _Doṣa-viruddha_ in frank Vāta states and dry (rūkṣa) constitutions; _avasthā-viruddha_ in dehydration, and in the fluid-depleted convalescent — the classical Chinese caution against draining where there is nothing to drain, restated in dravyaguna terms; _mātrā-viruddha_ in prolonged high dose.
- *(F) Folk/regional:* the Fujianese-Taiwanese *四神湯* and Cantonese long-simmered soup registers treat fúlíng as an everyday food-grade Spleen-supporting ingredient; this is living folk dietetics, (F)-tier, and is the context in which most people actually consume it.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Mercury — the one serious item.* The historical *朱茯神 / 朱砂拌茯神* (cinnabar-dusted poria-spirit) is a *mercuric sulfide preparation* and must not be used; cinnabar-containing Chinese preparations are restricted or prohibited in most jurisdictions. This is a caution about a *processing form*, not about the fungus.
  - *Additive diuresis* — theoretical additive effect with pharmaceutical diuretics; and, as with any diuretic herb, a theoretical concern about *lithium* clearance and about electrolyte balance in patients on loop or thiazide diuretics. Theoretical, not demonstrated — flagged as such.
  - *Documented interactions otherwise: essentially none.* Fúlíng is notable for the near-absence of reported herb-drug interactions and adverse events in the modern literature, consistent with its classical 平和 reputation.
  - *Allergy* — rare reports of hypersensitivity to fungal materials; no specific pattern established for this species.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Poria _is absent from the Avicennan canon and from Ibn al-Bayṭār's_ al-Jāmiʿ_, whose ~1400 simples include the East Asian materials that actually reached medieval Arabic pharmacy by trade — musk, camphor, cinnamon, rhubarb, galangal — and not this one. A bulky subterranean fungus with no fragrance and no value per unit weight was not a plausible long-distance trade good, which is the concrete reason for its absence; and medieval Arabic pharmacy had, like the saṃhitās, little developed fungal materia medica to receive it. The placement below is reasoned extension on the humoral axis, flagged throughout._ See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Names:* no classical Arabic, Persian, or Urdu drug-name. Modern Arabic herbal commerce uses transliterations (*فوليج / بوريا*) of the Chinese or Latin names — not evidence of tradition.

*Reasoned mizāj:* *Temperate tending Cold, and Dry, 1st degree* — the humoral rendering of 平 (neutral) with a drying-draining tendency. A drug that is nearly temperate yet actively desiccant is an unusual humoral profile, and in the Tibb framework it would most naturally be classed among the mild *mujaffif* simples rather than with the frankly cold refrigerants.

*Reasoned afʿāl (actions):* *Mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic — the governing action); *mujaffif* (desiccant, drying excess moisture); *mufattiḥ* (deobstruent, mildly — opening the obstructed passages that retain fluid); *muqawwī al-maʿida* in a mild degree (the 健脾 analogue — digestion-supporting without heating); *munawwim khafīf* and *muqawwī al-qalb* in a mild degree (the _fúshén_ Heart-settling analogue).

*Reasoned istemālāt (indications):* *istisqā'* (dropsy/edema — the single closest classical Tibb indication, and the one a Tibb physician meeting this drug would reach for first); *amrāḍ al-balgham* (phlegmatic conditions, the humoral analogue of 痰飲); retention of urine and difficult micturition; *khafqān* (palpitations) and *araq* (insomnia) of the non-hot type.

*Reasoned mawāneʿ (contraindications):* dry (*yābis*) temperaments and states of depletion, in which a desiccant diuretic is contrary; the same logic as the classical Chinese fluid-vacuity caution.

*Reasoned muṣliḥ / badal:* a desiccant-draining simple of this class would conventionally be corrected with a moistening-nutritive adjuvant — *kathīrāʾ* (gum tragacanth), a little honey, or almond — and the plausible *badal* within the actual Tibb pharmacopoeia would be the diuretic seed-and-root simples of the *Sharbat-i Bazūrī* register (melon and cucumber seeds, *kāsnī* or chicory root). *Explicitly reasoned, not textual*: no classical _muṣliḥ_/_badal_ table lists this drug, because the drug is not in the corpus.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none, and none possible* — unavailable in the 7th-c. Ḥijāz.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

Fúlíng's formula range is too large to enumerate; the representative set, by action-category:
- *Water-draining:* *五苓散* (water accumulation with exterior pattern, _Shāng Hán Lùn_); *豬苓湯* (damp-heat with urinary difficulty); *真武湯* (yang-vacuity water flooding, with aconite); *防己黃耆湯*; *五皮散* (five-peel powder, built on *茯苓皮* for superficial edema — the rind-form in its own formula).
- *Spleen-tonifying:* *四君子湯* and its lineage — *六君子湯*, *香砂六君子湯*, *異功散*, *參苓白朮散* (with 薏苡仁, see `coix.typ`).
- *Phlegm-transforming:* *二陳湯* (the base phlegm-damp formula, with 半夏 and 陳皮 — see `tangerine-peel.typ`); *苓桂朮甘湯* (phlegm-fluid in the chest, palpitations, dizziness); *半夏白朮天麻湯*.
- *Spirit-calming (茯神):* *歸脾湯* (Heart-Spleen dual vacuity); *酸棗仁湯* (vexed insomnia, _Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_); *安神定志丸*.
- *Yin-tonifying with built-in drainage:* *六味地黃丸* and its derivatives (*杞菊地黃丸* — see `chrysanthemum.typ`, `goji-berry.typ`; *知柏地黃丸*; *八仙長壽丸*), where fúlíng is one of the 三瀉. See `rehmannia.typ`.
- *Dietetic preparations:* *茯苓餅 (fúlíng bǐng,* poria cakes — a Beijing speciality, thin pancakes of poria flour, historically an imperial-kitchen food); *四神湯 (sìshén tāng,* "four spirits soup" — Fujianese-Taiwanese soup of fúlíng (or 芡實), 蓮子, 山藥, and 薏苡仁, a staple Spleen-supporting household dish, see `coix.typ`); fúlíng in Cantonese *老火湯* long-simmered soups; *茯苓粥* (poria congee).

=== Ayurveda

*No classical Ayurvedic formula* — non-canonical substance with no Sanskrit name (see above); the saṃhitās' generally unfavourable treatment of fungi as food means there is not even an analogous classical category. Modern "medicinal mushroom" products marketed alongside Ayurveda are a 20th–21st-c. wellness category, not a dravyaguna formulary.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

*No classical Greco-Roman or European medical tradition.* Three registers are noted to keep the record straight:
- *The "China root" confusion:* 16th–18th-c. European pharmacy's _radix chinae_ ("China root") is *_Smilax china_*, imported via Portuguese and Spanish trade and used for syphilis, gout, and chronic skin disease — a sarsaparilla-class drug with nothing to do with poria. Modern English-language sources that call poria "China root" import a false pedigree; flagged.
- *American tuckahoe:* the independent subsistence-food use of the same fungus (see Nomenclature) — alimentary, not medical, and not transmitted.
- *Modern Western supplement register:* poria now appears in Western "medicinal mushroom" and TCM-derived supplement markets, typically as extract capsules. Post-classical, commercial, and no Western traditional standing.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

*No classical preparation* — see the Tibb section above. Modern Unani practice in South Asia has not adopted it.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Fúlíng is a *medicinal fungus used by indication*, not a staple food whose use tracks the consumer's climate — but it has a sharply specific *production* ecology, a genuinely climate-matched *logic of action*, and one substantial regional *dietetic* register, so the section is not empty. See `climates.typ`.

=== Warm-humid subtropical montane pine forest (the production zone)

Köppen Cfa/Cwa at elevation: warm wet summers, mild winters, acidic pine-forest soils. Yunnan (the traditional premium *雲苓*), Hubei, Anhui, Guangxi, Fujian. Production is now overwhelmingly from *inoculated pine stumps and billets* rather than wild digging. *The ecological-medical point:* the fungus grows in precisely the warm, damp, Spleen-loading conditions (外濕, external damp, per the 六淫 correspondence 濕→脾) whose characteristic pathology it is the classical drug for. This "grows in the damp, treats the damp" alignment is not mystical and not a doctrine of signatures — it is the unremarkable consequence of a damp-climate pharmacopoeia developing its damp-draining drugs from its own flora — but it is a real and recurrent pattern in this book, and fúlíng is its clearest fungal instance.

=== Hot-humid South China, Taiwan, and Southeast Asia (the dietetic register)

Köppen Cfa/Am: long hot humid season; external 濕熱; Pitta-Kapha with Spleen loading in dravyaguna terms. Here fúlíng is eaten as *food*, not taken as medicine: *四神湯* (with lotus seed, Chinese yam, and Job's tears — a quartet of gentle Spleen-supporting, damp-draining ingredients, see `coix.typ`), Cantonese *老火湯*, *茯苓粥*, and herbal-shop soup packets. *Cares:* the constitutive parameters are *long simmering* (the polysaccharide fraction is water-extracted slowly) and *combination with warming or neutral tonics* rather than use alone — the dietetic tradition instinctively builds the 補中有瀉 balance into the soup pot. Taken as a lone draining agent in a hot-damp climate it would be unbalanced; as one quarter of 四神湯 it is not.

=== Temperate East Asia (Japan and Korea — the Kampo register)

Köppen Cfa/Dfa. Kampo uses fúlíng heavily (*ブクリョウ*) within imported Chinese formulas — _Goreisan_ (五苓散), _Rikkunshito_ (六君子湯), _Keishibukuryogan_ (桂枝茯苓丸), _Hochuekkito_ — with the Japanese tradition's characteristic standardization of dose and formula composition. The register is pharmacological rather than dietetic; the Chinese food uses did not travel with it.

=== Climates where fuling is not traditional

South Asia, the Middle East, Europe, Africa, and the Americas have no traditional use — the Ayurvedic and Tibb absences treated above are geographic in origin, and the North American occurrence of the same fungus produced an unrelated food use, not a medicine. The drug's indications are climate-independent (edema and damp patterns occur everywhere); its tradition is not.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

The transposition issue for fúlíng is *not* geographic — a damp-draining drug is appropriate wherever a damp pattern presents, and the indication travels cleanly. It is one of *form and framing*. First, the Western supplement register sells poria *extract capsules as a standalone "medicinal mushroom"* for immune support or weight loss, detached from both the formula logic that pairs it with tonics and the soup logic that dilutes it into a food — which inverts the drug's entire classical use, where it is almost never a sole agent. That is a _krama_- and _mātrā_-order error rather than a _deśa_ one. Second, the *indication drift* from "drains damp where damp is accumulated" to "diuretic for water weight" turns a pattern-matched drug into an undifferentiated one, which is precisely the misuse the classical fluid-vacuity caution names. Seasonally, the damp-draining register suits the damp season and damp patterns; habitual year-round use in dry constitutions is the _kāla-viruddha_ parallel. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Fúlíng is used in decoction and powder at medicinal doses and as a soup ingredient; as a *food* the sclerotium is largely indigestible structural polysaccharide, so no meaningful per-100 g macronutrient profile applies. Its pharmacological interest is in two fractions: the *polysaccharides* (the bulk of the mass) and the *triterpenes* (the minor, lipophilic fraction, concentrated in the rind).

*Key constituents:*
- *Polysaccharides (~80–90% of dry weight)* — chiefly *pachyman (β-pachyman)*, a water-*insoluble* (1→3)-β-D-glucan with (1→6)-β branches. *A pharmacologically important detail:* native pachyman is largely insoluble and relatively inert; the *water-soluble, bioactive* derivatives studied for immunomodulation (*pachymaran*, carboxymethylated pachymaran) are typically produced by chemical modification or by extraction methods that debranch the glucan — so the immunological literature does not straightforwardly describe what a traditional decoction delivers. Flagged, because this gap is routinely elided in popular summaries.
- *Triterpenes (the rind-concentrated fraction)* — lanostane-type: *pachymic acid*, *dehydropachymic acid*, *dehydrotumulosic acid*, *tumulosic acid*, *polyporenic acid C*, *eburicoic acid*, and the *poricoic acids (A–H and related)*. These carry much of the anti-inflammatory, diuretic, and renoprotective activity in the modern literature, and are *markedly more abundant in 茯苓皮* (the rind) than in the white inner flesh — an unexpected modern vindication of the classical decision to treat the rind as a separate, more strongly draining drug.
- *Ergosterol*, chitin, small amounts of protein and minerals; trace histidine, choline, and adenine derivatives.#footnote[Phytochemistry: pachyman as an insoluble (1→3)-β-D-glucan and the solubility/bioactivity problem of native vs. modified pachymaran; the lanostane triterpene series (pachymic, dehydrotumulosic, poricoic acids) and their concentration in the rind (茯苓皮) relative to the inner flesh — standard _Poria cocos_ phytochemistry reviews; verify against current literature. The rind/triterpene correspondence with the classical 茯苓皮 drug-form distinction is noted as a convergence, not as a claim that the classical distinction was chemically motivated.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Diuresis — weaker evidence than the classical reputation implies, and worth saying so.* Animal studies report natriuretic and diuretic effects attributed to the triterpene fraction (with proposed action on renal sodium handling and on aquaporin expression), but results are *inconsistent across models*, and *controlled human evidence is essentially absent*. Given that 利水 is the drug's defining classical action, the honest position is that the mechanism is plausible and partially supported in animals, and clinically unverified in humans — not that "poria is a proven diuretic."
- *Renoprotection* — the *poricoic acids* are an active area of modern research, with preclinical signals in models of renal fibrosis and chronic kidney disease (TGF-β/Smad and Wnt pathway modulation reported). Preclinical only; of genuine interest, not yet clinical.
- *Immunomodulation* — the modified, water-soluble polysaccharides enhance macrophage and lymphocyte activity in vitro and in animals, with preclinical antitumour signals. Subject to the solubility caveat above: this literature largely concerns *modified* polysaccharides, not decoctions.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — triterpenes inhibit phospholipase A2 and inflammatory signalling in standard models; reasonably consistent evidence at the preclinical level.
- *Sedative / anxiolytic* — animal studies report sedative and anxiolytic-like effects, consistent with the classical *寧心安神* use of 茯神; human evidence is limited to formula-level studies (e.g. Kampo and TCM compound trials) from which the single drug's contribution cannot be isolated. Flagged.
- *Gut microbiota and metabolic endpoints* — a growing preclinical literature; preliminary.#footnote[Pharmacology: representative reviews on _Poria cocos_ triterpenes and polysaccharides — diuretic/natriuretic animal data (inconsistent; human controlled data absent), poricoic-acid renoprotection (preclinical, TGF-β/Smad and Wnt signalling), polysaccharide immunomodulation (predominantly with chemically modified, water-soluble derivatives), anti-inflammatory and sedative preclinical findings. Verify against current systematic reviews. The gap between the drug's defining classical diuretic reputation and the thinness of human diuretic evidence is stated deliberately rather than elided, per this book's honesty standard.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* fúlíng has *no established toxicological ceiling* and one of the cleanest safety profiles in the Chinese pharmacopoeia; no limiting toxic constituent has been identified in the fungus itself.
- *Decoction (白茯苓 / 茯苓塊):* *9–15 g/day* standard clinical range; *up to 30 g* in water-accumulation formulas; higher in some classical prescriptions.
- *茯苓皮 (rind):* *15–30 g* where superficial edema is the target.
- *茯神:* *9–15 g*, for the spirit-calming indication.
- *Dietetic use* (四神湯, congee, poria cakes): effectively unrestricted; food-grade.
- *Avoid:* *朱茯神 / cinnabar-processed forms* — mercury; not a caution but a prohibition.
- *Cautions:* frank fluid/yin vacuity without damp accumulation, dehydration, and prolonged high-dose use (the classical logic, above); theoretical additive effect with pharmaceutical diuretics and theoretical concern with lithium; no established pregnancy contraindication, and fúlíng appears in classical pregnancy formulas (*當歸芍藥散*), but concentrated modern extracts lack pregnancy safety data.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice;* formula selection and dosing in Chinese medicine are properly a practitioner's work rather than self-prescription.#footnote[Dosing: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ monographs for 茯苓, 茯苓皮, 茯神; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. Cinnabar restriction: modern pharmacopoeial and regulatory restrictions on mercury-containing Chinese preparations. Reference thresholds requiring current verification, not medical advice.]

